      "dom_id": "question-18636",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p=0.6 \\\\ -p-5q=7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p=0.6 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ -p-5q=7 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p=6 \\\\ -p-5q=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can easily solve for $p$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =6 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $q,$ we can substitute $p=3$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-p-5q & =7 \\\\ -3-5q & =7 \\\\ -5q & =10 \\\\ q & =\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ q & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=-2.$",
